        <ol>
                    <li id="cf-fn-1">See, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" class="offsite" target="_blank" href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/press/monetary/20140917c.htm">Policy Normalization Principles and Plans</a>,&rdquo; FOMC, September 17, 2014, and the&nbsp;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" class="offsite" target="_blank" href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/press/monetary/20170315a.htm">FOMC post-meeting statement of March 15, 2017</a>.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-1" aria-label="Return from footnote 1.">Return to 1</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-2">Currently, there are two openings on the Board of Governors and there will be another when Governor Daniel Tarullo steps down in early April.
                        <a href="#cf-fn-ret-2" aria-label="Return from footnote 2.">Return to 2</a>
                    </li>
                    <li id="cf-fn-3">It is also worth remembering that the FOMC projections are conditional on what each participant views as the appropriate policy path and not necessarily what the participant views as the most likely policy path. For a clear and very useful explanation of what the FOMC projections are and are not, see Ben S. Bernanke, &ldquo;<a rel="noopener noreferrer" class="offsite" target="_blank" href="https://www.brookings.edu/blog/ben-bernanke/2016/11/28/federal-reserve-economic-projections/">Federal Reserve Economic Projections: What Are They Good For?</a>&rdquo; Brookings Institution blog post, November 28, 2016.
